WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Two motors and a battery were $300 power a $15,000 wheelchair. This is First Principles Daily, episode 165. The motors are brushless, the pack is lithium, their combined factory price sits between $304 and $400, and the chair that carries them still has to pass a dealer and an insurance channel before anyone rides it. Nothing in that passage is an exotic alloy or a hidden law of physics. The added layers are certification, billing codes, small volume assemblies,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.500
and parts and outsider is not allowed to buy.

00:00:33.494 --> 00:00:37.854
Power wheelchairs reach people through a narrow set of durable medical equipment suppliers,

00:00:38.294 --> 00:00:41.754
whose model depends on Medicare and private insurance reimbursement.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:46.894
National seating and mobility and new motion together control the majority of United States

00:00:46.894 --> 00:00:50.794
supply, and each post annual revenue above $400 million.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:55.114
A manufacturer ships a chair that already meets the code requirements.

00:00:55.114 --> 00:01:00.834
The dealer then adds seating, programming, and documentation that trigger higher reimbursement tiers.

00:01:01.454 --> 00:01:02.934
Repairs walk the same path.

00:01:03.514 --> 00:01:11.114
Those two firms hold the parts and the service contracts, so an independent shop cannot obtain the motors, the controllers, or the harnesses.

00:01:11.594 --> 00:01:18.814
A 2022 survey by USPIRG found that 40% of users waited seven weeks or longer for a fix.

00:01:18.814 --> 00:01:26.514
States right to repair bills, including New York S.0.4500, passed in 2026, tried to force

00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:28.394
the release of documentation in parts.

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:31.614
The installed base still moves through the original channel.

00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:37.394
Commodity motors and batteries pick up regulatory clearance, custom seating molds, and dealer

00:01:37.394 --> 00:01:40.014
margins before they reach the person who needs the chair.

00:01:40.594 --> 00:01:43.274
The codes themselves add steps that multiply cost.

00:01:43.274 --> 00:01:50.834
under the healthcare common procedure coding system, HCPCS, code E2377 covers an

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:56.854
expandable controller and code E2310 covers actuator connections. Each one

00:01:56.854 --> 00:02:01.014
demands specific documentation of medical necessity before reimbursement flows.

00:02:01.774 --> 00:02:05.394
Dealers therefore keep large inventories of pre-certified configurations

00:02:05.394 --> 00:02:10.094
instead of ordering commodity parts when a chair is ordered. That stock ties up

00:02:10.094 --> 00:02:15.514
capital. The carrying cost is passed forward. When a motor fails, the documentation chain

00:02:15.514 --> 00:02:20.734
starts over, and the downtime stretches. The structure rewards the firm that holds both

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:25.374
the hardware and the paperwork, because an outsider who wants to repair must first obtain

00:02:25.374 --> 00:02:30.554
the clearances the original supplier already has. Small production runs keep the certification

00:02:30.554 --> 00:02:35.754
cost per chair high, which discourages a new entrant who might otherwise compete on price.

00:02:35.754 --> 00:02:41.396
the channel reinforces itself. Set the channel aside for a moment and price the

00:02:41.396 --> 00:02:47.296
atoms. The physical inputs that actually move the chair are 224 volt, 250 watt

00:02:47.296 --> 00:02:53.796
brushless motors, 124 volt lithium-ion pack of 10 to 20 amp hours, an aluminum or

00:02:53.796 --> 00:02:58.656
steel frame, and a basic electronic controller. If you could wave a magic wand

00:02:58.656 --> 00:03:02.796
and arrange those raw materials into the finished thing for free, the bill would

00:03:02.796 --> 00:03:08.096
be the price of the commodities alone. Factory pricing published by 1,000 mobility in

00:03:08.096 --> 00:03:16.656
October 2026 puts each motor at 45 to $80 and the battery at 120 to $250. Those motors

00:03:16.656 --> 00:03:21.636
are already built at scale for e-bikes, which is why the original equipment range sits there.

00:03:22.196 --> 00:03:28.536
Two motors then run from $90 to 160. Even at the top of its range, the pack is a standard

00:03:28.536 --> 00:03:32.196
24-volt module, interchangeable with a scooter pack.

00:03:32.656 --> 00:03:38.216
Frame tubing, aluminum or steel, cut and welded, adds another 100-200 dollars.

00:03:38.696 --> 00:03:43.436
A controller board and a basic harness close the electrical loop for perhaps $50 more.

00:03:44.096 --> 00:03:50.796
Add the high ends, 160 for the motors, 250 for the pack, 200 for the frame, 50 for the

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:51.716
controller and harness.

00:03:52.296 --> 00:03:54.356
The sum still stays under $450.

00:03:54.356 --> 00:03:59.896
dollars. Call that the magic wand number at the cautious top of the quoted ranges, about

00:03:59.896 --> 00:04:07.076
$450. A finished power wheelchair that routinely clears $15,000, divided by that $450

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:13.076
floor, carries a first principles ratio near $33. The ratio is a measurement of where the

00:04:13.076 --> 00:04:17.736
price sits. It is not a verdict on the people who design, fit, or service the chair.

00:04:18.316 --> 00:04:23.096
The largest slice of the multiplier sits in regulatory clearance, insurance coding, and

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:27.216
the dealer channel, not in the cost of shaping aluminum or winding copper.

00:04:27.836 --> 00:04:32.336
None of those later steps changes the atoms inside the motors or the cells inside the battery.

00:04:32.916 --> 00:04:35.296
Each step is real under the rules as they stand.

00:04:35.956 --> 00:04:40.616
A different cut of the same factory, not the retail price, shows how the invoice itself

00:04:40.616 --> 00:04:41.156
is built.

00:04:41.676 --> 00:04:47.456
Of the factory free on board price, raw materials account for 35 to 40 percent, electronics

00:04:47.456 --> 00:04:51.416
for 25 to 30 percent, and labor for 15 to 20 percent.

00:04:52.156 --> 00:04:59.236
Certification, small batch seating, and distribution then multiply that factory figure several more times before the chair reaches the user.

00:04:59.836 --> 00:05:06.136
The first principles ratio of near 33 is that later multiplication, stacked on a commodity floor,

00:05:06.696 --> 00:05:10.316
not a claim that the factory invoice is already 33 times the metal.

00:05:10.936 --> 00:05:16.216
What the ratio is counting after the parts leave the floor is the cost of Medicare coding approval,

00:05:16.216 --> 00:05:20.416
the cost of custom-molded seating that satisfies the documentation rules,

00:05:20.856 --> 00:05:23.896
the dealer margin required to keep service contracts alive,

00:05:24.316 --> 00:05:28.136
and the carrying cost of chairs that sit in a warehouse while paperwork clears.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.330
Open Source Work already shows one way around that stack.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:36.610
The Lafayette College wheelchair team built a prototype assist on two

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:42.790
O-Drive motors at $99 each, a 48-volt 10-amp-hour pack at $189,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:51.410
dollars, and an O-Drive controller at $159. Their bill of materials totaled $1327. An

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:56.310
instructables builder reached a complete chair for about $2,000 with off-the-shelf scooter

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.790
parts. Right-to-repair statutes now advancing in seven states would let independent technicians

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:07.030
and owners buy the motors and harnesses that manufacture is currently withhold. A larger

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:11.750
redesign has three moves and each one needs a policy change and a volume commitment before

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:16.790
the ratio can fall. First, standardize the controller interface so any certified

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:21.730
electronics package can plug into any frame. Second, publish the actuator and

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:29.130
harness penouts that codes E2310 through E2377 already assume. Third, shift

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.370
seating from one-off foam molds to modular adjustable modules that still

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.270
satisfy Medicare documentation. Without those moves, regulatory cost and

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.350
channel costs stay the binding constraint.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:45.730
One objection is that a medical device must clear a higher safety bar than a consumer scooter.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:51.290
The same motors and cells already pass underwriter's laboratories and CE testing when they are

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:52.250
sold for e-bikes.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:57.450
The extra wheelchair-specific requirements center on documentation of medical necessity,

00:06:58.010 --> 00:06:59.610
not on new material properties.

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:05.590
Another objection is that custom seating has to match an individual body, a modular frame

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:11.050
with adjustable mounting points and interchangeable cushions can still take that variation while the

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:17.050
core drive unit is built in larger batches. The open source chairs show that a small team can assemble

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.650
and test the electronics and the powertrain once the interface specifications are public.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:26.970
Scaling that approach means a manufacturer or a regulator releases the pin outs and the

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:32.430
firmware hoax that now sit behind service contracts. Independent fabricators could then build

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:37.950
replacement controllers or seating modules that still qualify for the same HCPSCS codes,

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:41.850
and the weight between failure and repair could shrink from weeks to days.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.610
The portable lesson is about permission, not a new material.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:50.810
A chair whose motors and battery already exist as e-bike commodities is a design thing,

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.750
so the first principles ratio has a job to do.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:55.770
The job is to locate the gap.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:00.190
Here the expensive part is the permission structure wrapped around those commodities.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:04.810
who may code the chair, who may stock the part, who may touch it when it fails.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:10.570
That measurement does not scold the clinician who fits a seat or the technician who answers a service call.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.710
Those tasks stay necessary.

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:16.030
It says the multiplier lives after the copper is wound.

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:19.470
Some prices should never be told as engineering stories.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.530
A scarce view, a captive audience, a brand.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:26.470
Those are market power and this show says so and moves on.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.250
This chair is mixed and the mix is the point.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:32.830
Two suppliers and a closed parts bin are market structure.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:37.110
Documentation that must exist before reimbursement moves is a rule.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.430
Under that rule, the steps are genuine.

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:45.170
A from scratch rethink has room only in the slice that is process, organization, or rules.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:47.510
Not in a pretend shortage of lithium.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:54.390
Naming the slice wrongly sends the next builder hunting for a lighter alloy when the binding constraint is a locked interface.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:09:01.850
Releasing the same parts under published interfaces would let repair cycles shrink from months to days without new material science.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:07.950
A pin out is a map of which wire does which job. A harness spec is that map for the actuators.

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:14.770
Modular cushions on adjustable mounts keep the fit to a person's body without sending every frame through a one-off mold.

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:18.170
None of that asks the cells to become a different chemistry.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.130
It asks someone to make the specification viable.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:25.170
so more than one pair of hands can meet a code that already exists.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:29.950
Prototypes have shown the drive unit can be built once the parts are on the open market.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:36.190
What a prototype cannot do alone is make that interface the ordinary path through the next reimbursement cycle.

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:39.010
That takes volume, committed in advance,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:43.630
large enough that the code expects the open pinout rather than a closed service contract.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:50.090
Who will publish the first open controller pinout that still satisfies the existing HCPCS codes?

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:54.730
and what volume commitment would make that pin out the default for the next medicare cycle.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.250
That's First Principles Daily for Today.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:00.030
If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:03.730
share it with someone who depreciate it and subscribe wherever you listen.

